'''aoul''' (ä′öl), ''n.'' [Russ. ''aulŭ'', a village (of the Caucasians).] Among the people of the Caucasus, a village or a village community; hence, a Tatar camp or encampment.
{{smaller block|The ''aoul'' consisted of about twenty tents, all constructed on the same model, and scattered about in sporadic fashion without the least regard to symmetry.
{{right|''D. M. Wallace'', Russia, p. 330.}}}}

'''à outrance''' (ä ö-troṅs′). [F.: see ''outrage''.] To excess or to the utmost; with extreme vehemence; without limitation or reserve: as, to fight ''à outrance''. Often, incorrectly, ''à l’outrance''.
